  "chapter": 807,
  "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
  "text": " \n                APPROVAL MEMORANDUM - No. 156 Chapter 807\n \n       MEMORANDUM filed with Assembly Bill Number 4637, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\n        limiting the  exceptions  to  certain  effluent  limitations  in\n        Nassau  and  Suffolk  counties  and  requiring  certain eligible\n        projects for state aid involving water pollution control revolv-\n        ing fund agreements to take county-wide or  regional  wastewater\n        planning into consideration when determining eligibility\"\n \n    APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  amend  the  Environmental Conservation Law (ECL) to\nremove  exceptions  for  backsliding  in  certain  special   groundwater\nprotection  areas  and  require  best  available technology with special\nemphasis on reducing nitrogen pollution. The more extensive  and  expen-\nsive  treatment  required under this bill would likely deter development\nin special groundwater protection areas on Long Island. Because many  of\nthese  areas  are  located  inland, the bill could unintentionally drive\ndevelopment closer to the shorelines and thereby increase nitrogen load-\ning in marine waters due to substantial inconsistency in required  tech-\nnologies across the various areas of Long Island.\n \n  Additionally,  a majority of facilities that would be impacted by this\nbill would be those with small wastewater treatment systems that are not\nlarge contributors of nitrogen to Long Island's surface and groundwater,\nsuch as nursing homes, apartment complexes, and small businesses.  Meet-\ning  the more stringent effluent limitations required under the bill and\nachieving \"best available technology\" would require  expensive  upgrades\nthat  would likely be cost-prohibitive for many of those entities. While\nI share the sponsor's goal of addressing  the  nitrogen  issue  on  Long\nIsland,  the potentially exorbitant cost of these upgrades is not justi-\nfied by the more modest improvement in water quality that  would  result\nunder this bill.\n \n  Fundamentally, the bill fails to define or appropriately constrain the\nterm \"best available technology with special emphasis on reducing nitro-\ngen  pollution,\"  as  this  term  is not defined in the ECL and the cost\nimplications for local government and businesses would vary based on how\nthis term is interpreted.\n \n  To address these potential outsized fiscal impacts to state and  local\ngovernments  and  small  businesses,  I  have reached agreement with the\nLegislature requiring consideration of  economic  feasibility  and  cost\neffectiveness  of the best available technology for wastewater treatment\nrequired under this bill.\n \n  This bill is approved.                         (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                                ______\n",
  "year": 2021
 },
